<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tables of Heraclea</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tabulae Heracleenses</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tables of Heraclea are bronze tablets found in 1732 near Heraclea in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucania">Lucania</a>, southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. One side carries Greek texts of the 4th century BCE recording surveys of the sacred lands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dionysus">Dionysus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena-polias">Athena Polias</a>; the other carries a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> law of the 1st century BCE on Roman municipal affairs. They are kept in the National Archaeological Museum of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tables-of-heraclea.jpg" alt="Tables of Heraclea" width="320" height="547" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fabien Bièvre-Perrin · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tavole_di_Eraclea_03.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
